# Regression script for taming a beast (see TameVerb / RecruitedOutcome).
# Run: dotnet run -- --playground --skip-childhood --debug --seed 42 --no-encounters --period noon \
#        --spawn-beast wolf --observe-only wolf --cli-timeout 600 --cli-script cli/tame_beast.cli 2>&1 | grep '^\[cli\]'
# A non-zero exit means an `expect`/`wait` failed.
#
# Three flags make this reachable at all: --spawn-beast puts a wolf in the opening area (a factory
# rolls one 10-40% of the time and then lets it roam), --observe-only pins the observation to it, and
# `goal` pins the playground's goal draw to the verb under test — appease first, since a beast that
# still wants to kill you cannot be tamed, then tame.

wait
click menu New
wait mode ProtagonistCreation 20
click continue
wait mode WorldView 60
travel here
wait mode LocationInteraction 60
advance 12 180

# The beast is there and hostile: the phase opens on it under the threat rule.
expect wolf
state

# --- appease: enemy -> suspicious, which is what tame requires --------------------
strategy succeed
goal appease
click keyword wolf
choose 0
choose 0
advance 12 180
click action 0
choose 0
advance 12 180
click continue
advance 12 180
expect Appeasement

# --- close the segment; the beast is still in the scene and still observable ------
click button
wait 60
advance 12 180
expect wolf

# --- tame: the beast joins the party ---------------------------------------------
# Difficulty 4 off a three-modus-mentis chain: `strategy succeed` asks for more sixes
# than there are dice, which used to hang the game outright (NarrativeController caps it).
goal tame
click keyword wolf
choose 0
choose 0
advance 12 180
click action 0
choose 0
advance 12 180
click continue
advance 12 180
expect Joined you

# --- after: gone from the scene, present in the party ----------------------------
# The next segment re-applies the period (StartObservationPhaseWithHistory → ApplyTimePeriod),
# which re-places the scene's NPCs — the tamed beast is no longer among them, so it cannot be
# observed or acted on again.
#
# That half is NOT asserted on screen: `expect` scans the whole terminal, and the beast's name
# is all over the greyed history of the two segments that just tamed it. The signal is on stdout
# instead — with --observe-only still pinned to the beast, this phase prints
#   [debug] --observe-only 'wolf': nothing here matches — offering the whole scene.
# and `state` drops one observable (observed=1/10 before the tame, 3/9 after).
goal none
click button
wait 60
advance 12 180
state

# The party side is assertable: the management screen lists the beast under P A R T Y.
manage
wait 20
expect Stormtusk
manage
wait 20

# --- and it stays gone: leave the location, come back, the scene is rebuilt ------
# The rebuild is a pure function of the location id, so without the departed record in
# LocationInstanceState the very same wolf would be standing here again. stdout shows
#   SceneFactory: 'Stormtusk' left this location for good — not rebuilt
# (or the --spawn-beast equivalent, since that flag honours the same record).
click button
wait mode WorldView 120
advance 12 120
travel here
wait mode LocationInteraction 120
advance 12 180
state
quit
